WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Hey, everybody.

2
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.720
Welcome to Roll Back.

3
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.800
What is Roll Back?

4
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.150
I'm so glad you asked.

5
00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.320
Roll Back is where we share the best conversations

6
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.610
from our pre-video audio only catalog with you

7
00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.240
right here on YouTube for the very first time.

8
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.110
And today we bring you the story of the man

9
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.050
who fast talked his way into a recording contract

10
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.050
right out of college, the man who co-created

11
00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.430
and sold companies like Marquis Jet and ZICO Coconut Water,

12
00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.940
the guy who co-owns the Atlanta Hawks NBA franchise

13
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.780
with a few friends, and freely shares his wisdom

14
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.090
and his experience in a very entertaining way

15
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.350
on his highly popular and quite addictive Instagram account

16
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.020
inspiring millions of people across the world.

17
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.070
I'm talking of course

18
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.580
about the one, the only, Jesse Itzler.

19
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.320
Jesse is quite the character.

20
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.460
He eats only fruit before noon, he runs 100 mile races,

21
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.800
he raises millions for charity,

22
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.590
and when he isn't playing super dad to his four kids,

23
00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.170
he's an in demand motivational speaker

24
00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.310
with a life highlighted by one predominant theme,

25
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.680
never be afraid to fail.

26
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.190
And despite all of his many successes, in 2010,

27
00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.560
Jesse felt that his life had kind of settled

28
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.770
into a too comfortable routine.

29
00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.580
So he did what any rational human would do, right?

30
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.210
He invited a Navy Seal to move in with him,

31
00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.910
but this was no ordinary Navy Seal,

32
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.550
this was the one and only David Goggins.

33
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.550
And Goggins accepted Jesse's invitation on one condition,

34
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.860
and that condition was for 31 days,

35
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.690
Jesse had to do every single thing

36
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.060
that David asked him to do, no exceptions.

37
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.930
And the result was Jesse's book Living with the Seal,

38
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:56.580
which was the subject of this exchange from episode 197

39
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:01.580
that was recorded way, way back in November of 2015.

40
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.540 line:15%
So please hit that subscribe button and enjoy this very old,

41
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.113 line:15%
but still very relevant conversation with Jesse Itzler.

42
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.340
I've been wanting to meet you for a long time, man.

43
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.960
So this is super cool.

44
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.960
I feel like you're like this sort of Gen X version of Zelig,

45
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.800
you've lived all these different lives

46
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.823
and different chapters in many different ways in a really-

47
00:02:25.823 --> 00:02:27.060
We have that in common.

48
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:27.893
In a cool way.

49
00:02:27.893 --> 00:02:30.570
You know, I was trying to think

50
00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.897
as I was driving over here, I was like,

51
00:02:31.897 --> 00:02:35.160
"What is like the overarching sort of theme

52
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.527
or marching order of your life?"

53
00:02:36.527 --> 00:02:39.120
And it really boils down to, I think,

54
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.630
and correct me if I'm wrong, but being fearless,

55
00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.600
not being afraid to fail, and allowing yourself

56
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.580
to get out of your comfort zone,

57
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.880
which is obviously a predominant theme in the new book.

58
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.050
Yeah, no, definitely.

59
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.580
I think that sums it up pretty good

60
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:54.990
and doing things differently,

61
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:57.450
just not intentionally differently,

62
00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.780
but just looking at challenges and saying, "You know what?

63
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.180
that would be a great on my life resume."

64
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.250
The resume that you refuse to write down though.

65
00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.390
Correct.

66
00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.800
You be like, that's the thing, right?

67
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.030
You've never had a resume.

68
00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:09.863
Right, right.

69
00:03:09.863 --> 00:03:12.467
Just a life resume in your mind.

70
00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:13.587
Yeah, cool
Yeah.

71
00:03:13.587 --> 00:03:15.510
So, I mean, where do you think that...

72
00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.490
Where does that germinate from?

73
00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:18.829
I mean, were your parents like that?

74
00:03:18.829 --> 00:03:22.410
Where did that kind of sensibility come from?

75
00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:23.243
Well, first of all,

76
00:03:23.243 --> 00:03:25.290
it's a lot more fun living outside,

77
00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.000
living outside of your comfort zone

78
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.530
and trying different things.

79
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:33.030
But I've always been a check the box guy.

80
00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.270
You know, finish one thing and not really dwell on,

81
00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.730
oh, that was a good accomplishment or whatever

82
00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.470
and just look for the next challenge.

83
00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.470
I've always just always wanted to kind of keep going

84
00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.570
and keep trying to find new things that would excite me.

85
00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:46.980
I get bored easily.

86
00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.980
So I'm one of the, I guess, so many of us need a goal

87
00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.640
or something to train for.

88
00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.890
So I'm always looking for the next thing,

89
00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.650
whether it's business or physical or whatever.

90
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.900
Were you like hyperactive as a kid?

91
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.300
Did you have trouble like sitting down in class?

92
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.480
I didn't recognize that at the time,

93
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.504
but now that I look back on it, for sure.

94
00:04:08.504 --> 00:04:09.337
Yeah, yeah.

95
00:04:09.337 --> 00:04:11.160
You would've probably been medicated if you were a kid now.

96
00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.720
Yeah, I know, I know.

97
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:13.950
For not being able to focus

98
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:14.783
and everything like that.

99
00:04:14.783 --> 00:04:17.846
Well, I mean, we can track back

100
00:04:17.846 --> 00:04:19.920
and go through the music career

101
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.110
and all your kind of entrepreneurial ventures,

102
00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:26.940
but let's camp out in the book right now.

103
00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:27.773
Sure.

104
00:04:27.773 --> 00:04:30.038
Tell me a little bit about the book

105
00:04:30.038 --> 00:04:32.460
and kind of how this whole thing got sparked.

106
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.560
Sure, well, the book's called Living with a Seal

107
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.560
and I was at a 24 hour race in San Diego a couple years ago

108
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.850
and I was running the entire race

109
00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.670
as part of a six person relay team with friends.

110
00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.440
And the guy next to me,

111
00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.020
literally set up his little station next to me,

112
00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.240
was running the entire race alone.

113
00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.510
And he caught my eye because he was about 265 pounds,

114
00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:58.530
which is a lot of weight to carry in a 24 hour race

115
00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.000
that you're running by yourself.

116
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.200
And the race was unsupported,

117
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.120
so we had to bring all of our own supplies.

118
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.920
And at the time I just sold the company

119
00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.840
and I probably overdid my supplies,

120
00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.650
I had a tent, a masseuse, bananas,

121
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.630
like I think Whole Foods pulled up.

122
00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.260
And this guy next to me literally sat in a folding chair

123
00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.750
with a bottle of water and a bag of crackers

124
00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:19.980
with his arms crossed

125
00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.340
with this don't even think about messing with me look.

126
00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.380
And I was just like, "Who is this guy?"

127
00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.020
And at mile 70, he had broken all the small bones

128
00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.780
in both of his feet and had kidney failure.

129
00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:35.160
And I watched this guy, literally just on pure guts,

130
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.750
finish this race.

131
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.620
And I was like, "Who in the world is this?"

132
00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.990
So I Googled them, he had a fascinating backstory,

133
00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.670
which we can get to if you want.

134
00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.080
And I decided I wanted to meet him.

135
00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.990
I cold called him and I literally just picked up the phone,

136
00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.240
called him, he wasn't super chatty.

137
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.800
So I said, you know what?

138
00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.030
Yeah.

139
00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.830
Let me get on a plane and come see you.

140
00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.437
And after about 10 minutes of just sitting with him, I said,

141
00:05:59.437 --> 00:06:01.560
"My life would be a lot better

142
00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.900
if whatever he had psychologically,

143
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.470
whatever made this guy tick, rubbed off on me a little bit."

144
00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.410
And I invited him to move in with my family and I

145
00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.590
and three days later, he was at our breakfast table.

146
00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:14.850
And the idea was,

147
00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:17.310
he's gonna live with you for 31 days

148
00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.340
and the only rule is that you've gotta do everything

149
00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:22.620
that he says or he asks of you.

150
00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.050
Yeah, and I wanted to get in great shape.

151
00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.050
At the time, I was in a good routine

152
00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.820
or I thought it was a good routine.

153
00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.610
And so many of us live our life on routine

154
00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:36.390
and routines are great, but there definitely could be a rut.

155
00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.950
And I wasn't getting better.

156
00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.650
My routine was great, but I wasn't improving

157
00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.300
in any of the buckets in my life.

158
00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:44.550
But you're such a high performer

159
00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.140
in so many other areas of your life.

160
00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.570
Like your bar is already set pretty high

161
00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.880
in comparison to the average person.

162
00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.717
So to me, it takes a deep level of self-awareness to go,

163
00:06:55.717 --> 00:06:58.830
"No, there's still room there."

164
00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.030
Yeah, I mean,

165
00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.580
at the time I was doing a bunch of marathons,

166
00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:03.990
my times weren't getting better,

167
00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.480
my training, wasn't I was just doing it.

168
00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:07.620
I was going through the motions,

169
00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.767
but I wasn't at all giving it my all

170
00:07:10.767 --> 00:07:12.930
and I couldn't get that mojo, it's hard.

171
00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:16.020
You know, the one thing that he really taught me,

172
00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.840
and I'm sure you can relate to this

173
00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.550
through everything you've done,

174
00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:24.660
is we're all disciplined in our own ways,

175
00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.060
but to be consistently disciplined,

176
00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.820
consistently disciplined, is so hard.

177
00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:30.990
That's the hardest part.

178
00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:35.100
Right, and he was the most of the most

179
00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.250
of the highest level of consistency and discipline.

180
00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.540
His name is David Goggins.

181
00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.940
And since in the book, I referred him as Seal,

182
00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.770
but his name is David Goggins.

183
00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:47.280
And he came in and he just turned everything upside down

184
00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:48.113
from day one.

185
00:07:48.113 --> 00:07:49.950
And how much of it was purely

186
00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.780
like I just want to get in physical,

187
00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:55.290
great physical condition, versus beyond that,

188
00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:59.220
like sort of the mental discipline, the emotional discipline

189
00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.380
that would spill over into other areas of your life?

190
00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.030
Like were you thinking about that at the time

191
00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:06.000
or was this purely like a fitness bootcamp?

192
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.800
When I first kind of put my hand out

193
00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.237
and shook his hand across from the table and said,

194
00:08:10.237 --> 00:08:13.080
"He'll move in with me," it was predominantly,

195
00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.340
I wanted to get in great shape,

196
00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.210
but after two hours of him at my house,

197
00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:21.960
it flip flopped and the psychological side of it

198
00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.900
and what makes him tick and how is he so driven

199
00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.600
became 80 to 90% of it.

200
00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.190
So I knew I was gonna get in good shape,

201
00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:32.100
that was unavoidable, I mean, he's a machine,

202
00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.170
but I really wanted so much more

203
00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.420
because you go in and outta shape,

204
00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.730
you go through these peaks and valleys, up and down,

205
00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:41.010
but the mental side of it,

206
00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:43.080
you can really can last a lifetime.

207
00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:43.913
Right.

208
00:08:43.913 --> 00:08:46.170
And I got all these different little sound bites

209
00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.790
and tidbits of living with him.

210
00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:49.560
I mean, the first day that he came here

211
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.020
in two hours into our journey, we went down,

212
00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.180
he wanted to see how many pull-ups I could do.

213
00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:55.380
And by the way,

214
00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.080
he has set the Guinness Book of World's Records

215
00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.880
for most pull-ups in a day on 4,030 in 17 hours.

216
00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:04.920
So pull-ups for him weren't that difficult.

217
00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:08.070
Pull-ups for me, a 200 pound guy,

218
00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.570
that doesn't do any, was very tough.

219
00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:11.520
So I squeezed like eight.

220
00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:12.990
He said, "Alright, get off the bar

221
00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.180
and try it again in 30 seconds."

222
00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.940
Got back on the bar, I did like maybe six.

223
00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.310
He said, "One more time, wait 30 seconds, and try it again."

224
00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.950
And I barely got four and he turned

225
00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.470
and I was done, like lactic acid built up,

226
00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.930
my arms were like at a 90 degree angle.

227
00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.030
And he turned to me and he said,

228
00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.240
"All right, we're not leaving here

229
00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.100
until you do 100 more."

230
00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.550
And I said, "I can't possibly do 100.

231
00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.040
Like impossible for me to physically,

232
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:39.077
I might wanna do it, I can't do it."

233
00:09:39.077 --> 00:09:40.950
And he said, "No, you can do it.

234
00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:42.060
And I'm gonna prove it to you.

235
00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:45.060
We're not leaving this gym until you do it."

236
00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.280
So I would do one, walk around the gym, do another,

237
00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.780
but what he taught me and I did it,

238
00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.360
but what he taught me is we all have so much more

239
00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.300
in our reserve tank than we think we have.

240
00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:55.800
And his saying, one of your saying

241
00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.930
that has resonated with you

242
00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:01.110
is that when your brain tells you you're done,

243
00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:03.600
you're really only 40% done.

244
00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.712
And he taught me that right there.

245
00:10:05.712 --> 00:10:06.545
He kind of-

246
00:10:06.545 --> 00:10:09.180
Yeah, I had heard him say that one time

247
00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.230
and I never forgot it.

248
00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:13.020
And as we were kind of chatting before the podcast,

249
00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:16.138
that's something that I use in the talks that I give

250
00:10:16.138 --> 00:10:20.244
and also it was a big sort of mantra

251
00:10:20.244 --> 00:10:23.460
when I was doing EPIC5, when I would hit those low moments.

252
00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.400
And it's so true, it's so incredibly true

253
00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.050
and it's incredibly powerful.

254
00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.480
And when I'm reading your book,

255
00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:33.150
you refer to him as Seal, he's anonymous.

256
00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:34.230
But to me, immediate, I'm like,

257
00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:35.400
I know immediately,

258
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:37.160
there's the only person this could be is David Goggins.

259
00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:37.993
Right, right.

260
00:10:37.993 --> 00:10:39.960
Like anybody in the endurance community

261
