522
00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:47.010
that's peppered throughout the book is this idea

523
00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:49.950
that it's less about what you're doing,

524
00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:51.867
it's when you're doing it and how you're doing it.

525
00:20:51.867 --> 00:20:55.530
And that like commitment to doing whatever you're doing,

526
00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:57.660
however mundane it is, to do it the best

527
00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:01.020
that you can do with this relentless focus,

528
00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:04.440
like the ability of him to like be super present

529
00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:07.320
in whatever he is doing without any distraction.

530
00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:08.153
Right.

531
00:21:08.153 --> 00:21:10.110
Now part of that is he has the luxury

532
00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:11.940
of having a super simple life, right?

533
00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:14.473
He doesn't have a lot of distractions and...

534
00:21:14.473 --> 00:21:16.410
Yeah, he's not on Facebook or Twitter.

535
00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:17.243
No, no

536
00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:21.360
Why would he do that when he could be doing pushups?

537
00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:22.210
Exactly.

538
00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:24.420
But that's part of it.

539
00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:29.070
But I always felt like during our days

540
00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:33.254
that we were operating with 27 or 28 hours in a day,

541
00:21:33.254 --> 00:21:35.130
I was so more efficient at work,

542
00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.590
even though I was doing so much more physically

543
00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:39.780
and we were training so long,

544
00:21:39.780 --> 00:21:41.310
I was getting so much more done at work

545
00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:44.340
because all the non-essential things

546
00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:46.590
that he would just be like, "Eliminate 'em."

547
00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:48.373
Meetings, maybe I would take that.

548
00:21:48.373 --> 00:21:50.550
I didn't eat lunches, this and that.

549
00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:52.080
And he would find, you know,

550
00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:54.120
a lot of us think if we don't work out in the morning,

551
00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:56.850
ah, I miss my workout, I'll do it tomorrow.

552
00:21:56.850 --> 00:21:58.950
If we had a 15 minute break during,

553
00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:00.360
he shadowed me everywhere I went,

554
00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:03.780
he came to every meeting, every meal, everything.

555
00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:05.862
And if they'd be like, let's take 15 minute break

556
00:22:05.862 --> 00:22:07.680
to check emails and go to the bathroom.

557
00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.340
He'd be like, "Let's go do as many burpees

558
00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:11.160
as we can in 10 minutes

559
00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.830
and then take five minutes to do your stupid emails."

560
00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:15.030
Yeah, and there was that time

561
00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:17.040
where he made you do that during a break

562
00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.320
and like a negotiation or a business meeting

563
00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:20.850
and you're in a suit and you have to come back all sweaty.

564
00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:22.410
Yeah, yeah.

565
00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:25.440
Just finding that little sliver of extra time

566
00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:26.273
throughout your day.

567
00:22:26.273 --> 00:22:28.110
And you're always working out.
And he didn't care.

568
00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:30.360
Like it's not like you worked out and you're done,

569
00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:35.070
like it's a constant search for that little moment of time

570
00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:37.620
that you could maximize and use more efficiently.

571
00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:38.670
And he did.

572
00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:40.140
You know, if we were traveling

573
00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:41.460
and we would get home at 11 o'clock

574
00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:43.890
and he felt like the day wasn't complete,

575
00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:45.660
like we didn't get enough done,

576
00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:48.180
he would say, "All right, we'd still have 50 minutes left.

577
00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:50.970
We're gonna maximize those 50 minutes in the day."

578
00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:52.110
Were you game throughout?

579
00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:53.437
Was there ever a moment where you're like,

580
00:22:53.437 --> 00:22:55.530
"All right, man, come on."

581
00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:57.930
Or a breaking point where you're like,

582
00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:00.680
it's too much or you thought you weren't gonna make it?

583
00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:04.050
Several times, several times.

584
00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:06.540
And I just didn't wanna let him down.

585
00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:08.730
You know, it was just so motivating.

586
00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:10.770
It's one thing to read about inspiration,

587
00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:13.050
it's another thing to live with inspiration.

588
00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:15.600
And when I was just being around him

589
00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:17.490
and knowing what he's done, like you said,

590
00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:22.080
taping his feet to a bike and all this crazy stuff,

591
00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:24.060
I can't go out for a 50 minute run?

592
00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:26.453
I mean, it was just impossible to say no to him.

593
00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:30.363
And then after two or three weeks, when it became habit,

594
00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:33.120
I would look forward to it.

595
00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.280
I'd be like, "Oh, I can't wait for it to suck today."

596
00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:36.390
Interesting.

597
00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:39.837
And so it was really interesting.

598
00:23:39.837 --> 00:23:42.240
And I would bring 'em to these business meetings.

599
00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:44.790
And I remember specifically,

600
00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:46.679
we went to meet with Kevin Garnett,

601
00:23:46.679 --> 00:23:49.020
who at the time was playing with the Celtics,

602
00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:51.777
who was a Gatorade endorser, you know,

603
00:23:51.777 --> 00:23:53.370
he was an endorsed for Gatorade.

604
00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:56.711
But he was also really into what he puts in his body

605
00:23:56.711 --> 00:23:58.560
and his contract was coming up.

606
00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:01.140
And we thought for my coconut water company, ZICO,

607
00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:03.150
he'd be a great fit as an endorser.

608
00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:07.500
So I brought Goggins with me and we go to pitch in Boston,

609
00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:09.937
Garnett, and I introduced them and I said,

610
00:24:09.937 --> 00:24:11.820
"Oh, this is David Goggins, he's a Navy Seal.

611
00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:13.500
He's living with me to train me."

612
00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:14.640
And for the next two hours,

613
00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:18.600
those guys just went back and forth about fitness.

614
00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:20.970
You know, what they put in their body,

615
00:24:20.970 --> 00:24:22.590
food, this and that.

616
00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:24.900
And then in the end, they gave each other a bear hug

617
00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:26.220
and they said, "All right, great meeting."

618
00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:27.510
And I'm like, "What about ZICO?"

619
00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:28.957
And Garnett turned to me and he said,

620
00:24:28.957 --> 00:24:31.890
"Look, whatever you guys are doing, I'm in."

621
00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:32.723
So he was like the-

622
00:24:32.723 --> 00:24:33.556
The ultimate closer.

623
00:24:33.556 --> 00:24:34.590
Without question.

624
00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:35.423
Guys were so fascinated with him.

625
00:24:35.423 --> 00:24:38.040
I was, was like, I wish I could bring him everywhere.

626
00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:39.240
Just an incredible accessory

627
00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:41.160
that you could bring into the boardroom.

628
00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:43.290
No, there's you paint that scene really beautifully,

629
00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:45.146
'cause it starts off you being nervous

630
00:24:45.146 --> 00:24:47.651
about how this is gonna go and this negotiation,

631
00:24:47.651 --> 00:24:50.550
you bring David in, and immediately,

632
00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:53.049
like Kevin is in this sort of staring contest,

633
00:24:53.049 --> 00:24:54.903
like trying to figure out who this David guy is,

634
00:24:54.903 --> 00:24:56.910
he's not hearing anything that you're saying.

635
00:24:56.910 --> 00:24:58.590
Nothing.

636
00:24:58.590 --> 00:24:59.580
Right.
It's really great.

637
00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:00.690
It's Hysterical.

638
00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:01.980
And I'm going through a whole pitch,

639
00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:03.150
and then he just turns to Goggins.

640
00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:05.430
He's like, "So what do you do during a day?"

641
00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:08.100
You know, that was it, the floodgate's open.

642
00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:10.950
And those guys had their conversation and...

643
00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:11.820
I don't think there was anybody

644
00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:13.860
who could beat David in a staring contest.

645
00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:14.970
No.
No way.

646
00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:16.143
No.
Right?

647
00:25:16.143 --> 00:25:17.430
(Rich laughs)

648
00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:20.097
And also I love the part about like sort of just walking,

649
00:25:20.097 --> 00:25:22.350
like walking across midtown

650
00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:24.510
and you're wearing like 50 pound weight vests

651
00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:26.940
and he's got his eyes, it's like, he's in Fallujah.

652
00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:28.650
You know, he's like spotting,

653
00:25:28.650 --> 00:25:30.210
we gotta across the street,

654
00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:34.230
like looking for enemy sightlines the whole time.

655
00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:36.540
Yeah, he had these, we got two weight vests

656
00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:38.040
that we would wear when and walk to work.

657
00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:40.374
I would wear a suit somedays, not often.

658
00:25:40.374 --> 00:25:42.000
You're wearing the weight vest

659
00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.140
underneath your suit?

660
00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:43.980
Yeah, underneath it.

661
00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:45.450
And some days over it, but usually under.

662
00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:47.400
But sometimes it would be exposed

663
00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:49.451
and we look like we're gonna go blow up a building.

664
00:25:49.451 --> 00:25:50.284
I mean, he looks-

665
00:25:50.284 --> 00:25:52.230
Did anybody ever stop you?

666
00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:53.850
People, they couldn't stop us,

667
00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:55.710
'cause they run to the other side of the street.

668
00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:56.910
I mean, they were freaked out.

669
00:25:56.910 --> 00:25:57.743
I was freaked out.

670
00:25:57.743 --> 00:25:59.142
I thought like it was unsafe.

671
00:25:59.142 --> 00:26:01.635
This is 2010, right?

672
00:26:01.635 --> 00:26:04.230
When this was happening.
Yeah, yeah.

673
00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:05.063
Yeah.

674
00:26:05.063 --> 00:26:07.980
So he loved the weight vest.

675
00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:11.070
He'd wear the weight vest like he would put it on

676
00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:14.640
like he was putting on the jacket after he won the masters.

677
00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:15.750
I mean celebrate it.

678
00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:17.850
He would welcome it, he would sleep in it,

679
00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:21.510
he loved, he just loved challenge.

680
00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:22.800
It couldn't be hard enough.

681
00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:26.430
And he, as I recall, I think he had a heart thing

682
00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:28.890
that was going on with him that took him out,

683
00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:30.711
like 'cause he didn't compete for a number of years.

684
00:26:30.711 --> 00:26:32.040
And then he was kind of gone.

685
00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:33.870
Like I didn't see him anywhere,

686
00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:35.010
I didn't see his name anywhere,

687
00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:36.720
I think that might have been around 2009

688
00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:37.570
or something like that.

689
00:26:37.570 --> 00:26:39.435
Did he have surgery or did they got rectified

690
00:26:39.435 --> 00:26:40.680
or how did that get?

691
00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:42.150
So he accomplished

692
00:26:42.150 --> 00:26:43.830
all these amazing physical feats

693
00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:46.440
with literally, I believe, like 60%,

694
00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:50.220
only 60% of capacity of his heart.

695
00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:54.493
He had a huge hole in his heart and he has sickle cell.

696
00:26:54.493 --> 00:26:55.440
Oh, I didn't know that.

697
00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:56.760
Yeah, he has sickle cell.

698
00:26:56.760 --> 00:27:00.000
So it's just an amazing story.

699
00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.346
I mean, anyone listening should Google David Goggins

700
00:27:02.346 --> 00:27:04.620
and get more about his story

701
00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:06.900
or a lot of it is in my book Living with a Seal,

702
00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:11.040
but it's just guys like this are rare

703
00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:15.030
and when you can pull things out of their journey

704
00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:16.260
to help your personal life,

705
00:27:16.260 --> 00:27:18.480
whether it's training or work, and I was fortunate,

706
00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:23.220
I got both out of it, it just makes you so much better.

707
00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:27.600
Yeah, it's a lost sensibility in our culture.

708
00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.790
You know, now it's all about the life hack

709
00:27:29.790 --> 00:27:33.690
or what's the shortest route to get to the end game,

710
00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:37.710
and here's a guy who's so fully committed to the process,

711
00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:39.600
the journey, he's actually seeking out

712
00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:43.530
the most difficult route as opposed to finding the shortcut.

713
00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:48.210
And in that, finding the thing to celebrate is overcome.

714
00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:50.520
You know, it's like if it's easy to do then what is that?

715
00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:51.810
What are you gaining out of that?

716
00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:54.390
It's only through confronting and overcoming

717
00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:56.670
and getting to the other side of that thing

718
00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.670
that you thought you could never do that enriches your life.

719
00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:01.470
And also I would just add one thing,

720
00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.390
so many of us have finish lines.

721
00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:04.920
I'm gonna run the New York Marathon,

722
00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:07.230
and when you train for that one specific thing,

723
00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:09.600
you celebrate it with a party with your friends,

724
00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.217
you hang the medal up and the pictures up and you said,

725
00:28:12.217 --> 00:28:13.650
"Okay, it's on my resume.

726
00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.150
I did the New York Marathon."

727
00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:16.410
He has no finish lines.

728
00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:17.820
No.
It's like, once that's done,

729
00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:19.470
what's the next race?

730
00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:21.540
You know, what's the next thing to do?

731
00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:22.380
Yeah, he was in 12th
Check the box.

732
00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:23.213
for one second.

733
00:28:23.213 --> 00:28:26.550
I think he did Ironman world championships one year,

734
00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:30.210
he parachuted into the swim, did the whole Ironman,

735
00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:32.190
and when it was done, he went out for a run.

736
00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:33.720
He's like, this is another workout.

737
00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:34.553
Right.

738
00:28:34.553 --> 00:28:35.670
Like he doesn't make too big of a deal

739
00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:36.709
out of anything.

740
00:28:36.709 --> 00:28:38.460
Right, right.

741
00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:41.226
So that's a valuable lesson as well.

742
00:28:41.226 --> 00:28:44.760
So what are like the biggest lessons

743
00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:46.230
that you take away from this?

744
00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:49.050
Like how has this impacted your life post David?

745
00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:50.733
I mean, it's been five years now,

746
00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:54.990
what are the biggest changes that have stuck with you

747
00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:58.170
that are still part of your daily life?

748
00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:01.090
Well, for starters, definitely the same,

749
00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:03.780
just to reiterate what you dwelled,

750
00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:06.540
you tapped into on one of your races, the 40% rule,

751
00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.880
something that definitely resonates with me,

752
00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.410
I always think about.

753
00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:14.580
But just on a daily basis, he's just motivated

754
00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:16.920
to live a lot more with a lot more grit.

755
00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:19.740
Grit is a great indicator of future success

756
00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:21.300
and he was the grittiest,

757
00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:23.940
but just to live my life with as much grit,

758
00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:26.070
look for challenges that maybe stuff

759
00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:28.680
that I don't think I can complete and try to do it,

760
00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:30.360
always have something on my calendar

761
00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:32.640
that I'm looking forward to, that I can train for,

762
00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:34.140
and then what's next after that?

763
00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:35.703
I'm building out a schedule.

764
00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:38.790
I'm way more efficient with my time.

765
00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:42.150
I've eliminated a lot of the non-essential stuff in my life.

766
00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:44.100
I'm trying to live more simply.

767
00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:46.850
My life's a little complicated, his life was so simple.

768
00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:50.610
And I think he didn't really want what I had

769
00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:52.530
and I wanted a lot of what he had

770
00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:54.390
without getting rid of what I had.

771
00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:58.260
Like I didn't want to downgrade my house or my apartment,

772
00:29:58.260 --> 00:29:59.490
but I wanted the simplicity.

773
00:29:59.490 --> 00:30:02.910
So I'm trying it to live a real simple,

774
00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:04.960
as simple as I can, and-

775
00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:06.600
Would what would be an example

776
00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:09.750
of something that maybe you cut loose?

777
00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:12.060
Well, I'm not a fancy guy,

778
00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:13.920
I'm not into fancy dinners.

779
00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:16.710
We've talked about I only fruit until noon,

780
00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:18.720
but even just wardrobe, you know?

781
00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:22.470
I mean, in my closet, as soon as he left,

782
00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:24.663
one of the first things I did was go through

783
00:30:24.663 --> 00:30:25.770
and say, "You know what?

784
00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:29.250
I only wanna have 20 things in my closet for this season."

785
00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:30.796
Instead of having a couple of jackets

786
00:30:30.796 --> 00:30:31.837
and a couple of this, I'm like,

787
00:30:31.837 --> 00:30:34.950
"I'm gonna take the two that I like, the two sweaters,

788
00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:37.440
the t-shirts, the running shorts, and that's it."

789
00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:39.120
And I loved it.

790
00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:40.290
It felt so clean.

791
00:30:40.290 --> 00:30:45.290
And it felt even that one little small little change

792
00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:47.400
opened up stuff at work.

793
00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:50.130
I felt it just opened up a theme that stuck with me

794
00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:51.630
about just simplifying things

795
00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:55.920
and that I didn't feel like I had to return every email,

796
00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:57.840
I didn't feel like emails owned me,

797
00:30:57.840 --> 00:30:59.310
I took control of my own email.

798
00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:02.010
So I didn't feel like I had a respond in five minutes

799
00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:04.410
to every email and be beholden to that.

800
00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:06.780
Or, oh, this guy's gonna get mad

801
00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:07.727
if I don't respond to the email, it's like-

802
00:31:07.727 --> 00:31:09.033
That's a big one.

803
00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:11.520
What gives someone the right?

804
00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:14.520
That someone emails me, what gives them-

805
00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:16.820
Now what gives them the right to think

806
00:31:16.820 --> 00:31:19.593
that I have to email them back immediately?

807
00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:22.530
Why do I have to stop what I'm doing with my kids

808
00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.510
or my family and return that email?

809
00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:26.910
Shouldn't that answer be on my terms?

810
00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:31.027
And pre-Goggins, I would be like,

811
00:31:31.027 --> 00:31:32.880
"Oh man, so I gotta get back to him right away.

812
00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:33.930
Da, da, da."

813
00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:36.063
Post-Goggins, I was like, "You know what?

814
00:31:36.063 --> 00:31:38.040
I'm gonna put me first."

815
00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:42.450
Putting me first was a big takeaway from him.

816
00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:44.730
Yeah, so you've kind of created this pie chart

817
00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:48.010
about how you allocate your time every day, right?

818
00:31:48.010 --> 00:31:50.130
Tell me what that looks like.

819
00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:55.050
Well, I draw a circle and I start with,

820
00:31:55.050 --> 00:31:56.640
okay, I need seven hours of sleep,

821
00:31:56.640 --> 00:31:59.670
that's kind of where I'm comfortable operating on seven,

822
00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:02.490
six isn't good for me, eight's probably a little too much.

823
00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.157
And I said, "Okay, I have 17 hours left in a day.

824
00:32:04.157 --> 00:32:06.090
How do I wanna spend it?"

825
00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:08.970
And I literally put three hours for me.

826
00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:11.310
And that three hours of time could be a run,

827
00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:13.960
it could be sitting in a sauna, it could be doing emails,

828
00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:16.050
it could be doing watching TV,

829
00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:18.000
but the Jesse time is something

830
00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:20.040
that I definitely prioritize.

831
00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:22.440
And then I have time, a certain amount allocated time

832
00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:24.690
with my wife and my family,

833
00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:27.420
and the rest is pretty much work.

834
00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:28.740
And that's how I break up my day.

835
00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:30.150
And I approach it,

836
00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:32.640
it's pretty much the same breakdown every day,

837
00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:35.400
but I do change it depending on what's going on,

838
00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.741
but I always make sure that I have three hours for me

839
00:32:38.741 --> 00:32:42.417
and a certain amount of time for my wife and my kids.

840
00:32:42.417 --> 00:32:43.830
And so does the training fall

841
00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:45.390
into that three hours?

842
00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:47.250
If you're gearing up for a race?

843
00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:49.650
If I'm gearing up, I might extend it a little bit.

844
00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:50.730
You know, usually training,

845
00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:52.620
I usually actually keep as a separate,

846
00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:54.930
the three hours usually doesn't even include the training

847
00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:58.200
because again, if you're efficient with your time, 24 hours,

848
00:32:58.200 --> 00:33:00.537
try to fill up on a pie chart 24 hours.

849
00:33:00.537 --> 00:33:03.930
If seven of it's sleep, you still have 17 hours.

850
00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:08.250
If you're efficient at work and you work X amount of hours,

851
00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:09.870
you still have a lot of time.

852
00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:10.703
And you know what?

853
00:33:10.703 --> 00:33:11.730
Get up an hour earlier.

854
00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:12.900
Right, right, right.

855
00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:15.660
Yeah, that's something that I'm always trying

856
00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:17.137
to find balance with that, you know?

857
00:33:17.137 --> 00:33:21.210
And I feel like I vacillate between extremes.

858
00:33:21.210 --> 00:33:23.100
Like in the meta, there's balance,

859
00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:24.930
like if you look back over the course of a year,

860
00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:27.900
my life looks really balanced, but on a day to day basis,

861
00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.310
trying to get that mix right,

862
00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:32.804
like to achieve that pie chart every single day

863
00:33:32.804 --> 00:33:35.250
is something that is always alluding me.

864
00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:37.110
But even if you're working a full-time job

865
00:33:37.110 --> 00:33:39.510
and you work eight hours a day and you sleep six,

866
00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:42.540
you have 10 hours.

867
00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:45.570
You have 10 hours to allocate.

868
00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:47.580
So even if I I'm trying to just figure out now

869
00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:48.603
how I even do it.

870
00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:51.930
Even if you ran three hours a day

871
00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:53.700
and spent three hours by yourself

872
00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:55.050
and three hours with your family,

873
00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:55.883
you still have an hour.

874
00:33:55.883 --> 00:33:57.360
Yeah.

875
00:33:57.360 --> 00:33:59.160
I mean, there's a lot of time

876
00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:00.570
if you don't dilly-dally.

877
00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:01.403
Right.

878
00:34:01.403 --> 00:34:03.780
And he didn't dilly-dally.

879
00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:05.160
There was no dilly-dallying.

880
00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:06.510
The dilly-dally would be like, you know what?

881
00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:09.540
I got downtime, I'm gonna do some sit ups.

882
00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:10.980
Everything was maximized.

883
00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:12.330
Now of course it's unrealistic

884
00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:15.150
for us to all live our life to that extent.

885
00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:18.213
But there's pieces of it we can pull out to get better.

886
00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:21.690
Do you feel like you were able

887
00:34:21.690 --> 00:34:24.870
to crack his exterior

888
00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:28.410
and figure out what it is that drives him?

889
00:34:28.410 --> 00:34:29.760
Like what's behind that,

890
00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:32.550
that has created this person?

891
00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:33.477
Well, I talk about it with him

892
00:34:33.477 --> 00:34:35.400
and I talk to him about it

893
00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:40.050
because it's so unique and his motivator,

894
00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:43.140
and we all have our own motivation, his motivator was anger.

895
00:34:43.140 --> 00:34:45.155
He had a really tough childhood,

896
00:34:45.155 --> 00:34:47.490
he has a lot of anger inside,

897
00:34:47.490 --> 00:34:50.610
but he's been able to channel that on a daily basis

898
00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:52.530
whenever he needs it.

899
00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:56.073
And that's really what drives him.

900
00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:00.780
He likes to look at his life as like a wet rag

901
00:35:00.780 --> 00:35:04.384
that you kind of squeeze out all the water.

902
00:35:04.384 --> 00:35:07.980
And he looks at the rag like it's his soul.

903
00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:12.060
And he wants to squeeze out every single drip drop of water

904
00:35:12.060 --> 00:35:13.620
out of his soul to know

905
00:35:13.620 --> 00:35:15.843
that he's maximized his time on Earth.

906
00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:18.120
But I think in some respects,

907
00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:21.538
it can be said that when your fuel is anger,

908
00:35:21.538 --> 00:35:24.860
that's an unsustainable fuel source, right?

909
00:35:24.860 --> 00:35:26.250
You're gonna burn out.

910
00:35:26.250 --> 00:35:29.550
Like at some point, is there a way to confront that fear,

911
00:35:29.550 --> 00:35:31.230
work through it, get to the other side,

912
00:35:31.230 --> 00:35:35.490
and be able to operate from a different place?

913
00:35:35.490 --> 00:35:38.460
And did you sense, that he holds onto that?

914
00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:40.350
Like if he was actually to confront that

915
00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:42.930
and work through it, that suddenly he wouldn't be the person

916
00:35:42.930 --> 00:35:45.430
that he is anymore or he wouldn't have that drive?

917
00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:49.320
Well, I think he definitely still taps

918
00:35:49.320 --> 00:35:50.153
into the anger.

919
00:35:50.153 --> 00:35:51.360
He might be the exception to the rule,

920
00:35:51.360 --> 00:35:52.860
he might be that one asterisk,

921
00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:54.593
like on the bottom
He can go forever on that.

922
00:35:54.593 --> 00:35:55.710
of the page.

923
00:35:55.710 --> 00:35:57.390
You know, this doesn't apply to everyone.

924
00:35:57.390 --> 00:35:59.583
Asterisks: it does apply to David Goggins.

925
00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:02.880
I think he's also set up,

926
00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:07.080
just he has such control of his mind.

927
00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:09.510
He's convinced himself that he can just do it

928
00:36:09.510 --> 00:36:12.870
or he is gonna try to do it no matter what.

929
00:36:12.870 --> 00:36:16.189
And again, it goes back to being the consistency

930
00:36:16.189 --> 00:36:20.850
and the discipline, he's consistently convinced himself

931
00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:24.000
that he's up for the task, that he can do it,

932
00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:26.820
that he was put on Earth to see what he's made of,

933
00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:31.820
and so for him, maybe the anger is something

934
00:36:34.230 --> 00:36:35.484
that that he can still hold onto

935
00:36:35.484 --> 00:36:39.360
and maybe it is something that won't go away, I don't know.

936
00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:41.430
And maybe that's channeled with other emotions

937
00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:42.893
or other things or just the will to be the best

938
00:36:42.893 --> 00:36:46.230
or whatever it is, but whatever that potion is

939
00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:47.640
inside of him, it's working.

940
00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:48.480
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

941
00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.111
Well, the other interesting thing about the book

942
00:36:50.111 --> 00:36:52.201
is it really is peppered

943
00:36:52.201 --> 00:36:54.750
with a lot of business advice, you know?

944
00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:56.850
It's not just, "Oh, here's how I got in shape

945
00:36:56.850 --> 00:36:58.110
and here's the workouts that I did

946
00:36:58.110 --> 00:36:59.370
and here's the pearls of wisdom

947
00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:00.570
that David was dropping on me."

948
00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:03.175
And sort of aside from him kind of

949
00:37:03.175 --> 00:37:07.440
being your accessory in these business meetings,

950
00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:09.750
there was some wisdom that you were able to kind of call

951
00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:11.430
from hanging out with him

952
00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:14.280
that has applied to your entrepreneurial life.

953
00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:15.120
Yeah.

954
00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:18.990
You know, I've had multiple gigs.

955
00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:21.120
I had a private jet company called Marquis Jet

956
00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:24.120
and I had no experience or background in private aviation,

957
00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:25.909
I was in the music business,

958
00:37:25.909 --> 00:37:28.410
I was a partner in ZICO Coconut Water.

959
00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:31.500
So multiple, and there's always been a big learning curve.

960
00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:35.370
I didn't study to be in any of those fields.

961
00:37:35.370 --> 00:37:36.203
I don't think you can.

962
00:37:36.203 --> 00:37:38.190
And like we said, I've never had a resume.

963
00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:42.210
So I've always been like had a combination

964
00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:43.710
of let me get my foot in the door

965
00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:45.180
and kind of figure the rest out later.

966
00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:46.380
Let me get the deal

967
00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:48.870
and I'll figure out how to make the deal work.

968
00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:51.330
So the first step has always been for me, getting the deal,

969
00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:53.850
getting the right partners, hiring my weaknesses,

970
00:37:53.850 --> 00:37:57.120
surrounding myself in filling the positions

971
00:37:57.120 --> 00:37:58.710
that I didn't wanna play,

972
00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:03.180
but it's always been mixed with a real passion

973
00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:05.730
for stuff that I wanted to learn more about,

974
00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:09.333
or get better at, or whatever.

975
00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:13.800
But there's no secret sauce, you know?

976
00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:18.663
It's the ability to not be scared to fail, to call audibles,

977
00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:24.000
and just when you do fail,

978
00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:25.350
just know that you're just getting a little nudge

979
00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:28.050
that you're off course and get back on course somehow.

980
00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:28.883
Yeah.

981
00:38:28.883 --> 00:38:31.800
I mean, tracking back to the beginning of your career,

982
00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:34.623
you have this sort of audacity to, I guess,

983
00:38:34.623 --> 00:38:36.989
is it fair to say bluff your way into

984
00:38:36.989 --> 00:38:39.390
the music industry
That's fair.

985
00:38:39.390 --> 00:38:43.380
as Jesse James, the rapper, and then take that opportunity

986
00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:46.620
once you're in the door to then translate it into,

987
00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:49.080
you had a song that was on the Billboard 100, right?

988
00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:50.697
Yeah.
And then realizing

989
00:38:50.697 --> 00:38:54.420
there was this niche in the sports arena field

990
00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:56.880
of writing these songs for these franchises

991
00:38:56.880 --> 00:39:00.150
and turning that into like a full blown business.

992
00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:01.140
Yes.

993
00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:04.310
So do you think that...

994
00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:09.120
I'm trying to figure out how to articulate this.

995
00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:13.860
That your kind of audacity is just innate

996
00:39:13.860 --> 00:39:16.233
or do you think that that can be taught?

997
00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:21.210
I mean, that fearlessness to be able to kind of walk

998
00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:23.070
into a room without a resume

999
00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:25.863
and sort of secure the desired result?

1000
00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:28.650
Well, I always had the confidence

1001
00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:33.480
that if I could get into the room and connect the dots,

1002
00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:35.340
and put myself in the game,

1003
00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:38.250
that I could figure out how to get to the fourth quarter.

1004
00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:41.070
And when I got my record deal,

1005
00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:45.150
I faced rejection probably 20 times.

1006
00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:48.120
I sent out, like everybody trying to get a record deal

1007
00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:51.720
in the '80s, a cassette with a three song demo

1008
00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:55.440
to all the various record labels in New York and LA

1009
00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:57.090
and didn't hear one single word back.

1010
00:39:57.090 --> 00:40:00.210
And then I would call these labels and nothing.

1011
00:40:00.210 --> 00:40:01.320
And I didn't have a lawyer.

1012
00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:03.330
My dad owned a plumbing supply house,

1013
00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:05.100
I didn't have a powerful lawyer.

1014
00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:07.080
I'm like, "How in the world am I gonna get a record deal?"

1015
00:40:07.080 --> 00:40:07.920
Like this is crazy.

1016
00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:10.410
And one night I was at the studio in New York

1017
00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:12.343
and one of my favorite rappers of the '80s,

1018
00:40:12.343 --> 00:40:15.720
a guy named Dana Dane had an advanced cassette

1019
00:40:15.720 --> 00:40:19.110
of his second album that no one had sitting on the board,

1020
00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:20.013
the music board.

1021
00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:23.670
So I borrowed it, I put it in my pocket,

1022
00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:24.660
hey, I just wanted to hear it.

1023
00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:28.230
I was a fan of his, but when I went out to LA after college,

1024
00:40:28.230 --> 00:40:30.390
I had read that the owners of a record label

1025
00:40:30.390 --> 00:40:32.760
called Delicious Vinyl, which is a big independent,

1026
00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:35.670
probably the hottest independent label

1027
00:40:35.670 --> 00:40:38.460
in the early '90s, they had a song called Bust A Move,

1028
00:40:38.460 --> 00:40:41.280
Young MC, and Wild Thing from Tone Loc.

1029
00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:43.980
The founders loved Dana Dane.

1030
00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:48.980
So when I called up, I basically threw a lot of confusion,

1031
00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:52.980
convinced them or led them to believe that I was Dana Dane

1032
00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:54.420
and I set up a meeting with the owner

1033
00:40:54.420 --> 00:40:56.370
who was a huge fan of Dana's.

1034
00:40:56.370 --> 00:40:59.100
And they were referring me as Dana on the phone

1035
00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:01.747
and I was responding like, "Yes, you know."

1036
00:41:01.747 --> 00:41:04.170
"Dana, you can come at 2:00 to see Mike."

1037
00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:05.370
I was like, "Great, I'll be there."

1038
00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:07.470
And then when I rang the buzzer,

1039
00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:09.300
I said, "I'm Dana here to see Mike."

1040
00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:11.550
Dana's an African American guy from Brooklyn,

1041
00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:13.323
I'm a white guy from Long Island.

1042
00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:18.000
So obviously they didn't know what was what, but anyway,

1043
00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:20.070
I get all the way into this guy's office.

1044
00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:22.190
So the goal was to just get

1045
00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:23.640
in the office no matter what.

1046
00:41:23.640 --> 00:41:25.570
Get in the office and handcuff myself

1047
00:41:25.570 --> 00:41:28.380
until I get a deal or something, something.

1048
00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:29.983
So first you've gotta get over this hurdle

1049
00:41:29.983 --> 00:41:31.410
of this guy being upset

1050
00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:33.677
that you're not who you said you were, right?

1051
00:41:33.677 --> 00:41:34.510
Right, right.

1052
00:41:34.510 --> 00:41:35.370
So how does that go?

1053
00:41:35.370 --> 00:41:37.410
Well, so he comes in, by the way,

1054
00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:38.790
he's now a great friend of mine,

1055
00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:40.140
he signed my wedding license,

1056
00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:41.187
this guy's become a great friend,

1057
00:41:41.187 --> 00:41:44.490
and Dana is also a great friend, but no,

1058
00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:46.333
so I'm sitting in the office and the secretary comes in

1059
00:41:46.333 --> 00:41:48.480
and says, "Dana, can I get you some water?"

1060
00:41:48.480 --> 00:41:50.250
I said, "Thank you, that'd be great."

1061
00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:51.150
They bring water in.

1062
00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:52.710
And I'm waiting for Mike Ross,

1063
00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:54.300
the owner of this label to come in, and he comes in,

1064
00:41:54.300 --> 00:41:55.483
he says, "Who the hell are you?

1065
00:41:55.483 --> 00:41:57.150
Where's Dana?"

1066
00:41:57.150 --> 00:41:59.460
I said, "Oh, Dana's running a little bit late."

1067
00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:01.410
I said, "But I have his cassette."

1068
00:42:01.410 --> 00:42:02.243
You know, why don't we put it in?

1069
00:42:02.243 --> 00:42:04.110
I said, in fact, I have my cassette too.

1070
00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:05.640
He's like, "What are you talking about?"

1071
00:42:05.640 --> 00:42:08.100
I said, "Oh, so I've been making music with Dana for years,"

1072
00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:10.360
just started really confusing him even more

1073
00:42:11.430 --> 00:42:14.010
until finally he let me put my cassette in.

1074
00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:16.260
And I had a song called College Girls

1075
00:42:16.260 --> 00:42:19.080
and he listened to about 30 seconds of it.

1076
00:42:19.080 --> 00:42:20.370
I'd already-

1077
00:42:20.370 --> 00:42:21.203
That's a long time.

1078
00:42:21.203 --> 00:42:22.036
A long time.

1079
00:42:22.036 --> 00:42:23.490
And I'd already built a little bit of credibility,

1080
00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:25.350
'cause I had this cassette,

1081
00:42:25.350 --> 00:42:27.540
I knew a lot of the right names

1082
00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:30.060
and I'd been around the scene a little bit

1083
00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.503
and he stopped my song and he said,

1084
00:42:32.503 --> 00:42:35.220
"You know, this is great.

1085
00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:37.620
Would you be willing to give this song to Tone Loc,

1086
00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:41.730
one of his artists, and I didn't,

1087
00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:45.650
but it led to dialogue and going down the path

1088
00:42:45.650 --> 00:42:47.400
and ultimately, my record deal.

1089
00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:48.810
Right, that's amazing.

1090
00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:51.510
And is it that same sort of spirit

1091
00:42:51.510 --> 00:42:54.240
that you took to Marquis Jet?

1092
00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:55.073
Everything.

1093
00:42:55.073 --> 00:42:57.210
You know, how did that happen?

1094
00:42:57.210 --> 00:43:00.270
Everything in my life has been like that.

1095
00:43:00.270 --> 00:43:01.680
Marquis Jet, similar thing.

1096
00:43:01.680 --> 00:43:05.070
You know, I was a guest on a private jet,

1097
00:43:05.070 --> 00:43:06.180
loved the experience,

1098
00:43:06.180 --> 00:43:08.640
my partner and I were literally sitting

1099
00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:11.940
in the back of a friend of ours who invited us on a trip

1100
00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:13.050
on one of his planes.

1101
00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:16.050
And we were like, this is unbelievable.

1102
00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:18.450
How do we fly like this more often?

1103
00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:22.740
And came up with this idea for a 25 hour flight card,

1104
00:43:22.740 --> 00:43:24.810
which ultimately became Marquis Jet,

1105
00:43:24.810 --> 00:43:27.450
and had this great, we really thought

1106
00:43:27.450 --> 00:43:29.430
that there would be a huge market for people

1107
00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:32.130
that would wanna buy this 25 hour prepaid card.

1108
00:43:32.130 --> 00:43:34.020
The problem was we had no airplanes.

1109
00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:37.590
So we had to go to NetJets who was the 800 pound gorilla.

1110
00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:41.460
And I gotten a call about a year prior to that

1111
00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:42.746
from a friend of mine

1112
00:43:42.746 --> 00:43:46.290
who wanted to put one of his friend's daughters,

1113
00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:48.810
wanted to get tickets to a Christina Aguilera concert

1114
00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:50.190
and knew that I knew the manager

1115
00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:51.963
from my days in the music business.

1116
00:43:53.587 --> 00:43:55.080
And I didn't know who the guy was,

1117
00:43:55.080 --> 00:43:58.380
but not only did we get this gal, the 16 year old,

1118
00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:02.190
for her birthday tickets, she had the opportunity

1119
00:44:02.190 --> 00:44:03.807
to be a background singer on this studio,

1120
00:44:03.807 --> 00:44:06.180
the guy was gonna do me a favor, the manager.

1121
00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:08.305
And it turns out that the fellow

1122
00:44:08.305 --> 00:44:11.460
whose daughter it was, this guy, Jim Jacobs,

1123
00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:13.500
who was the president of NetJets.

1124
00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:16.230
So when we had this idea a year later, I called.

1125
00:44:16.230 --> 00:44:17.063
Now, he doesn't know me.

1126
00:44:17.063 --> 00:44:18.840
I was like the the behind the scenes guy

1127
00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:20.460
that facilitated this thing.

1128
00:44:20.460 --> 00:44:21.600
I get him on the phone.

1129
00:44:21.600 --> 00:44:23.367
Again, just, he had no idea what I was talking about.

1130
00:44:23.367 --> 00:44:24.780
I'm like, "Christina Aguilera."

1131
00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:25.740
He is like, "You're Aguilera?"

1132
00:44:25.740 --> 00:44:26.820
I'm like, "No."

1133
00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:28.530
You know, da, da, I'm like, "No, your daughter..."

1134
00:44:28.530 --> 00:44:29.610
Just started.

1135
00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:31.182
Harry Truman has this great line,

1136
00:44:31.182 --> 00:44:33.780
"If you can't convince 'em, confuse 'em."

1137
00:44:33.780 --> 00:44:34.860
So that's kind of been my-

1138
00:44:34.860 --> 00:44:36.780
Starting to get that this is your tactic.

1139
00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:38.910
Yeah, just get everyone super confused.

1140
00:44:38.910 --> 00:44:39.743
Yeah.

1141
00:44:39.743 --> 00:44:41.190
It led to a meeting.

1142
00:44:41.190 --> 00:44:45.810
And we took a meeting and presented our plan for Marquis Jet

1143
00:44:45.810 --> 00:44:48.450
and we were faced with the no.

1144
00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:50.700
You know, like, "This is not for us.

1145
00:44:50.700 --> 00:44:51.850
We're not gonna give two 30 year old kids,"

1146
00:44:51.850 --> 00:44:55.170
I was 30 at the time, "our fleet of airplanes

1147
00:44:55.170 --> 00:44:57.810
to try some kind of jet card program."

1148
00:44:57.810 --> 00:45:00.540
And we ended up going back a week later.

1149
00:45:00.540 --> 00:45:03.030
And instead of just taking that no,

1150
00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:06.690
we brought in our own focus group of athletes

1151
00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:10.140
and entertainers and agents that talked about,

1152
00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:12.750
one-by-one, got up, we literally set up chairs

1153
00:45:12.750 --> 00:45:14.250
like it was a focus group

1154
00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:16.410
and discussed why they would never buy a share

1155
00:45:16.410 --> 00:45:17.970
of an airplane or their own airplane,

1156
00:45:17.970 --> 00:45:20.400
but they would buy what we were proposing,

1157
00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:22.170
this 25 hour card.

1158
00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:24.930
And it ended up being a really big business.

1159
00:45:24.930 --> 00:45:25.763
That's interesting.

1160
00:45:25.763 --> 00:45:27.990
I mean, with that knowledge,

1161
00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:31.170
why wouldn't NetJets just create that internally?

1162
00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:33.117
I think two reasons,
Take that idea.

1163
00:45:33.117 --> 00:45:35.760
I think one: I don't think they really thought

1164
00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:37.920
it would be as big as it became.

1165
00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:40.996
And two: we were in that demo.

1166
00:45:40.996 --> 00:45:45.030
We could reach that young customer and the athlete

1167
00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:46.740
and talk to that guy better than they could.

1168
00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:49.230
They were set up really to sell corporate

1169
00:45:49.230 --> 00:45:51.840
and to high net worth older individuals.

1170
00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:54.060
We were selling to a much younger,

1171
00:45:54.060 --> 00:45:56.280
we were selling to the Kobe Bryants of the world.

1172
00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:58.237
And I think that they thought,

1173
00:45:58.237 --> 00:46:00.000
"Well, if there's gonna be any success,

1174
00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:02.520
let's see if these guys can sell to that world."

1175
00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:03.353
And we did.

1176
00:46:03.353 --> 00:46:05.940
Yeah, the one thing that you really seem to get

1177
00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:08.130
is the value and importance

1178
00:46:08.130 --> 00:46:10.695
of having that kind of celebrity endorser.

1179
00:46:10.695 --> 00:46:12.840
And that seems to be like a cornerstone

1180
00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:15.150
of every business that you get into is making sure

1181
00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:18.060
that it's sort of cool with those people, right?

1182
00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:19.350
When they're signing on for it,

1183
00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:22.710
that creates this ripple effect that then drives demand.

1184
00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:24.000
Yes and no, though.

1185
00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:26.250
The no to that is the product has to be great

1186
00:46:26.250 --> 00:46:28.980
because celebrity will get someone to buy the product once

1187
00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:30.510
and it'll get you on the shelves,

1188
00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:32.370
but it won't get 'em to buy it twice.

1189
00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:34.710
So you have to win 'em with great product.

1190
00:46:34.710 --> 00:46:36.750
Marquis Jet was a great product

1191
00:46:36.750 --> 00:46:39.300
and ZICO Coconut Water was a great product.

1192
00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:41.970
I love that story of how you chased

1193
00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:44.010
after Matt Damon and Ben Affleck

1194
00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:46.440
when you found out they were gonna be flying

1195
00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:49.415
on some other client of yours share

1196
00:46:49.415 --> 00:46:51.600
on their way to Sundance, right?

1197
00:46:51.600 --> 00:46:53.760
Yeah, we had just started Marquis Jet

1198
00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:56.860
and every day, I got a manifest of who was on the planes,

1199
00:46:56.860 --> 00:46:58.980
who was flying with us.

1200
00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:02.880
And Matt was a guest and Ben were guests of somebody else,

1201
00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:05.160
I saw their name, and I was in New York.

1202
00:47:05.160 --> 00:47:05.993
And I was like,

1203
00:47:05.993 --> 00:47:07.650
we were trying to build out the Hollywood vertical.

1204
00:47:07.650 --> 00:47:08.483
So I was like,

1205
00:47:08.483 --> 00:47:11.040
"I have to get them to sign up to our program.

1206
00:47:11.040 --> 00:47:12.480
So I literally was in the cab

1207
00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:15.630
and at the time I was running a lot, I was wearing shorts,

1208
00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:17.250
it was winter, I was in shorts,

1209
00:47:17.250 --> 00:47:19.110
I'm not even kidding, going to work.

1210
00:47:19.110 --> 00:47:21.540
And I said to the guy, "Take me to LaGuardia.

1211
00:47:21.540 --> 00:47:23.880
And I just got on a plane to LA

1212
00:47:23.880 --> 00:47:24.930
'cause their plane was flying

1213
00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:28.050
from LA to the Sundance Film Festival.

1214
00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:30.240
So I flew to LA, I met him on the plane,

1215
00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:31.967
I introduced myself as Jesse,

1216
00:47:31.967 --> 00:47:34.699
one of the co-founders of Marquis Jet and coincidence,

1217
00:47:34.699 --> 00:47:36.603
I'm going to Sundance also.

1218
00:47:37.530 --> 00:47:38.880
You know, do you mind if I guys...

1219
00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:41.880
They had a huge upgrade scheduling,

1220
00:47:41.880 --> 00:47:43.830
just on a fluke, they had a big plane.

1221
00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:47.010
So I could actually sit and kind of like with a staff

1222
00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:49.680
where to sit or on like a jump seat.

1223
00:47:49.680 --> 00:47:51.660
So I told 'em, "I was just gonna quietly jump up

1224
00:47:51.660 --> 00:47:52.860
if you needed me, da, da, da."

1225
00:47:52.860 --> 00:47:53.880
And then once we got in the air,

1226
00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:55.530
I started talking to them.

1227
00:47:55.530 --> 00:47:58.477
And Nate said to me that they were having,

1228
00:47:58.477 --> 00:47:59.400
"A nice to meet you."

1229
00:47:59.400 --> 00:48:00.603
We said, "Look, we're going

1230
00:48:00.603 --> 00:48:01.560
to this project green light release.

1231
00:48:01.560 --> 00:48:04.230
If you wanna come, you can come meet us.

1232
00:48:04.230 --> 00:48:06.420
Love to see you at the event at Sundance."

1233
00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:08.400
Now, first of all, I had no clothing,

1234
00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:11.580
I had no hotel, it's Sundance, everything is sold out,

1235
00:48:11.580 --> 00:48:14.100
it's freezing, I'm in shorts.

1236
00:48:14.100 --> 00:48:15.203
You have no hotel there

1237
00:48:15.203 --> 00:48:16.189
or anyplace to stay.
I have nothing.

1238
00:48:16.189 --> 00:48:17.022
Nothing.

1239
00:48:17.022 --> 00:48:17.910
And I'm like, "Oh, I got meetings.

1240
00:48:17.910 --> 00:48:19.200
You know, this whole thing."

1241
00:48:19.200 --> 00:48:20.130
And I get off the plane.

1242
00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:22.290
So I get some stuff,

1243
00:48:22.290 --> 00:48:24.810
I check into the hotel like 20 miles away.

1244
00:48:24.810 --> 00:48:25.770
I'm like, you know what?

1245
00:48:25.770 --> 00:48:28.380
I didn't close the deal, I gotta go to this thing.

1246
00:48:28.380 --> 00:48:30.150
So I show up to the project green light event,

1247
00:48:30.150 --> 00:48:32.130
there's 2000 people online.

1248
00:48:32.130 --> 00:48:34.470
I'm like, how am I gonna get into here?

1249
00:48:34.470 --> 00:48:36.450
So I went right up to the front of the line

1250
00:48:36.450 --> 00:48:38.280
and I said, "Hey guys, I'm here with Matt and Ben,

1251
00:48:38.280 --> 00:48:39.270
I'm on their advanced team

1252
00:48:39.270 --> 00:48:41.190
to make sure that the tables are set up."

1253
00:48:41.190 --> 00:48:43.743
The guy opened up the velvet rope, walked right in,

1254
00:48:44.790 --> 00:48:48.780
and then I went upstairs to the VIP table

1255
00:48:48.780 --> 00:48:50.645
and I took a black marker and I wrote my last name,

1256
00:48:50.645 --> 00:48:52.384
Itzler, on a piece of paper.

1257
00:48:52.384 --> 00:48:55.920
And I wrote reserved on another piece of paper.

1258
00:48:55.920 --> 00:49:00.210
And I put it on the tabletops next to Matt's table

1259
00:49:00.210 --> 00:49:02.397
where it said like the VIP thing.

1260
00:49:02.397 --> 00:49:06.540
And I literally walked in there and sat next to those guys.

1261
00:49:06.540 --> 00:49:10.230
And I asked them when they were flying home.

1262
00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:11.400
They said, "You know, we don't have anything booked yet."

1263
00:49:11.400 --> 00:49:13.260
I said, "Why don't you guys fly home with me?

1264
00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:14.940
Which I wasn't flying home.

1265
00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:17.910
And on the flight home, I sold them a card.

1266
00:49:17.910 --> 00:49:18.930
Right, there you go.

1267
00:49:18.930 --> 00:49:20.370
That's a great story, man.

1268
00:49:20.370 --> 00:49:21.990
Yeah, we laugh about it still.

1269
00:49:21.990 --> 00:49:24.210
The balls of that is incredible,

1270
00:49:24.210 --> 00:49:26.308
but did you tell them how the whole thing?

1271
00:49:26.308 --> 00:49:28.170
Oh yeah.
They know the story.

1272
00:49:28.170 --> 00:49:30.363
Oh yeah, now when I talk to those guys about it,

1273
00:49:30.363 --> 00:49:32.610
they crack up.

1274
00:49:32.610 --> 00:49:33.630
Yeah, that's fantastic.

1275
00:49:33.630 --> 00:49:34.463
I love that.

1276
00:49:34.463 --> 00:49:35.430
So-

1277
00:49:35.430 --> 00:49:36.870
When we got back on the plane,

1278
00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:39.420
Matt's manager said to me,

1279
00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:42.157
he said to turn to Matt on the plane ride home and he goes,

1280
00:49:42.157 --> 00:49:45.228
"What's the manager from the club doing on our plane?"

1281
00:49:45.228 --> 00:49:47.805
No one knew what was going on.

1282
00:49:47.805 --> 00:49:49.080
That's great, man.

1283
00:49:49.080 --> 00:49:52.770
So ultimately, you exit Marquis,

1284
00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:55.350
it sells to Berkshire Hathaway, right?

1285
00:49:55.350 --> 00:49:56.340
Yeah.

1286
00:49:56.340 --> 00:49:59.220
And at some point running enters the picture,

1287
00:49:59.220 --> 00:50:01.860
like when does that start to creep in?

1288
00:50:01.860 --> 00:50:03.720
So I decided, you know,

1289
00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:06.730
I ran this relay race years ago

1290
00:50:07.830 --> 00:50:10.470
in San Diego, a 24 hour relay.

1291
00:50:10.470 --> 00:50:12.120
And it was wild because I got

1292
00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:15.780
to see people run 100 miles by themselves,

1293
00:50:15.780 --> 00:50:18.180
which I never thought was even possible.

1294
00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:20.370
And I just was always planted a seed in my head

1295
00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:21.690
that it was something that I would love to see

1296
00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:23.490
if I was capable of doing.

1297
00:50:23.490 --> 00:50:26.370
And I decided I wanted to raise money

1298
00:50:26.370 --> 00:50:28.110
for a bunch of foundations

1299
00:50:28.110 --> 00:50:32.640
and I just signed up for this 24 hour race with 100.

1300
00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:34.773
I was gonna stop at 100 if I got there.

1301
00:50:36.000 --> 00:50:40.260
So I took 90 days off from work.

1302
00:50:40.260 --> 00:50:43.350
I wouldn't say off, but committed 90 days during work

1303
00:50:43.350 --> 00:50:44.430
to train for this race.

1304
00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:47.610
And I trained like, I mean, I was going berserk,

1305
00:50:47.610 --> 00:50:48.443
morning, night.

1306
00:50:48.443 --> 00:50:51.180
You know, I would work and then I would just to prepare

1307
00:50:51.180 --> 00:50:52.620
for the sleep deprivation.

1308
00:50:52.620 --> 00:50:55.530
I would work till 11 and then run till six in the morning

1309
00:50:55.530 --> 00:50:56.363
and then go back to work.

1310
00:50:56.363 --> 00:50:58.260
You know, all this stuff.

1311
00:50:58.260 --> 00:51:02.010
And so that was really where I got the bug.

1312
00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:06.087
And then running takes a lot, I'm a big 6'2" guy,

1313
00:51:06.087 --> 00:51:08.310
and I'm not really built like a runner,

1314
00:51:08.310 --> 00:51:11.310
but I got the endurance bug

1315
00:51:11.310 --> 00:51:13.620
and started doing some standup paddle races

1316
00:51:13.620 --> 00:51:17.160
and anything that just looked exciting.

1317
00:51:17.160 --> 00:51:17.993
Right.

1318
00:51:17.993 --> 00:51:20.883
And so how did that 100 miler go for you?

1319
00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:26.820
You know, I had a really good game plan going in.

1320
00:51:26.820 --> 00:51:30.330
I spent these 90 days talking to anyone

1321
00:51:30.330 --> 00:51:33.270
that'd ever run 100 miles, reading everything, hydration,

1322
00:51:33.270 --> 00:51:35.610
nutrition, had a great game plan.

1323
00:51:35.610 --> 00:51:38.460
And I stuck to it until I couldn't stick to it

1324
00:51:38.460 --> 00:51:40.080
because, as you know-

1325
00:51:40.080 --> 00:51:42.330
In 100 miles, it's gonna break loose on you.

1326
00:51:42.330 --> 00:51:44.027
It broke loose around 60.

1327
00:51:44.027 --> 00:51:46.713
And 75, it flew away.

1328
00:51:48.150 --> 00:51:50.880
But I gutted it out, but it put me in a wheelchair.

1329
00:51:50.880 --> 00:51:52.740
I was banged up.
Oh, wow.

1330
00:51:52.740 --> 00:51:54.930
My hips and joints were really banged up

1331
00:51:54.930 --> 00:51:56.730
and my feet were battered.

1332
00:51:56.730 --> 00:51:58.803
I mean, totally battered.

1333
00:51:59.820 --> 00:52:03.960
But this is the best thing I ever did in my life.

1334
00:52:03.960 --> 00:52:05.190
And you raised a bunch of money, right?

1335
00:52:05.190 --> 00:52:06.990
Like a million and a half bucks.

1336
00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:09.302
And this is for the foundation that you started, right?

1337
00:52:09.302 --> 00:52:10.170
Yep.

1338
00:52:10.170 --> 00:52:14.910
And I distributed it to 10 different US based charity,

1339
00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:16.590
charitable organizations.

1340
00:52:16.590 --> 00:52:19.057
And does the foundation still exist?

1341
00:52:19.057 --> 00:52:19.890
It does.

1342
00:52:19.890 --> 00:52:21.540
Like what's going on with that?

1343
00:52:21.540 --> 00:52:22.373
You know,

1344
00:52:22.373 --> 00:52:24.900
I'm new to this whole philanthropy stuff.

1345
00:52:24.900 --> 00:52:26.730
You know, I'm still trying to figure out

1346
00:52:26.730 --> 00:52:30.755
what the best way to deploy resources is.

1347
00:52:30.755 --> 00:52:32.820
So I'm going to a lot of conferences and listening

1348
00:52:32.820 --> 00:52:33.930
and talking to a lot of people,

1349
00:52:33.930 --> 00:52:35.613
but the foundation still exists.

1350
00:52:37.020 --> 00:52:38.190
100 Mile Foundation.

1351
00:52:38.190 --> 00:52:40.440
100 Mile Man Foundation, yeah.

1352
00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:44.730
And since then, I've done multiple 100 mile challenges

1353
00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:46.110
to raise money for the foundation.

1354
00:52:46.110 --> 00:52:48.600
And I also put my own money into the foundation,

1355
00:52:48.600 --> 00:52:51.120
but we did a 100 mile bike ride

1356
00:52:51.120 --> 00:52:53.811
called City to the Sand from New York City to the Hamptons.

1357
00:52:53.811 --> 00:52:57.060
And we had 100 CEOs that each raised 10 grand,

1358
00:52:57.060 --> 00:52:58.290
so a million dollars.

1359
00:52:58.290 --> 00:53:03.033
We've done a 100 mile spin thing in Central Park.

1360
00:53:04.980 --> 00:53:06.900
I've done a thing called Hell on the Hill.

1361
00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:09.810
In my backyard, I have a really big incline

1362
00:53:09.810 --> 00:53:14.810
in my house in Connecticut, it's about a 35% grade

1363
00:53:15.240 --> 00:53:18.360
that's about a little less than 100 yards.

1364
00:53:18.360 --> 00:53:20.370
And we go up and down 100 times,

1365
00:53:20.370 --> 00:53:22.260
about eight and a half miles.

1366
00:53:22.260 --> 00:53:23.700
But it's hard.

1367
00:53:23.700 --> 00:53:24.780
Really hard.
That's hard.

1368
00:53:24.780 --> 00:53:28.020
It's really brutally hard.

1369
00:53:28.020 --> 00:53:30.330
It only takes about, you know,

1370
00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:32.400
well actually Goggins won it this year.

1371
00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:36.300
He wanted a 1:55, but it takes a regular human anywhere

1372
00:53:36.300 --> 00:53:39.450
from three to four hours, but it is brutal.

1373
00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:40.283
Right.

1374
00:53:40.283 --> 00:53:42.360
And so what does running mean to you,

1375
00:53:42.360 --> 00:53:43.860
like in your life equation?

1376
00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:46.140
Like how does that impact your family life,

1377
00:53:46.140 --> 00:53:49.170
your entrepreneurial life?

1378
00:53:49.170 --> 00:53:50.970
Well, it's my form of meditation.

1379
00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:54.780
So if I don't run, I feel way worse.

1380
00:53:54.780 --> 00:53:56.730
It's not a good day if I don't run.

1381
00:53:56.730 --> 00:53:58.473
I'm just not connected to myself.

1382
00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:01.020
You know, I'm a little grumpy.

1383
00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:02.477
But it's become habit for me.

1384
00:54:02.477 --> 00:54:04.890
It's worked into my pie chart.

1385
00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:06.630
It's one of the things that I have to do.

1386
00:54:06.630 --> 00:54:09.033
And it doesn't have to be a run, it could be a paddle,

1387
00:54:09.033 --> 00:54:11.070
it could be burpees, it could be something,

1388
00:54:11.070 --> 00:54:13.860
but the running, you know, I don't listen to music,

1389
00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:15.663
it's a very spiritual thing for me.

1390
00:54:17.250 --> 00:54:19.890
And it's just my way of clearing my head

1391
00:54:19.890 --> 00:54:21.240
and starting my day.

1392
00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:22.650
Never any music.

1393
00:54:22.650 --> 00:54:23.483
Never.

1394
00:54:23.483 --> 00:54:25.375
And in the 100 miler?

1395
00:54:25.375 --> 00:54:28.980
I had a playlist that I knew I would probably need

1396
00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:31.020
to get me through some part of it.

1397
00:54:31.020 --> 00:54:33.560
And I put it on at mile 93,

1398
00:54:33.560 --> 00:54:35.430
So the last couple of miles, I took it off.

1399
00:54:35.430 --> 00:54:37.410
But no, I don't like to listen to music.

1400
00:54:37.410 --> 00:54:39.870
I like to be totally clear on my thoughts.

1401
00:54:39.870 --> 00:54:42.453
I do my best thinking when I'm running.

1402
00:54:43.800 --> 00:54:45.150
Lately, I've been run walking.

1403
00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:50.150
I've been walking too, which I love, and that's my time.

1404
00:54:50.550 --> 00:54:53.040
Where's the best place to run around here?

1405
00:54:53.040 --> 00:54:54.300
Well, now I'm in Atlanta.

1406
00:54:54.300 --> 00:54:56.070
I live in Atlanta full time.

1407
00:54:56.070 --> 00:55:01.070
And in my neighborhood, there's a ton of runners.

1408
00:55:01.691 --> 00:55:04.530
It's hilly, there's rolling hills,

1409
00:55:04.530 --> 00:55:06.690
there's a nice four mile loop.

1410
00:55:06.690 --> 00:55:07.523
So I do that a couple times.

1411
00:55:07.523 --> 00:55:08.730
There's some good hills.

1412
00:55:08.730 --> 00:55:10.200
And I love running steps.

1413
00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:12.690
So I'll go to the stadium and I'll just run steps

1414
00:55:12.690 --> 00:55:15.090
for an hour or something and I love that.

1415
00:55:15.090 --> 00:55:17.820
Well, that's a good time to good place

1416
00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:20.040
to segue into the Atlanta Hawks.

1417
00:55:20.040 --> 00:55:21.960
So this is new for you, right?

1418
00:55:21.960 --> 00:55:22.793
Yep.

1419
00:55:22.793 --> 00:55:23.910
Part owner of the Hawks.

1420
00:55:24.840 --> 00:55:26.310
Tell me what that's like.

1421
00:55:26.310 --> 00:55:30.810
Like, what does that mean in terms of your involvement

1422
00:55:30.810 --> 00:55:33.240
and your participation in the organization?

1423
00:55:33.240 --> 00:55:35.010
Well, I'm a huge basketball fan.

1424
00:55:35.010 --> 00:55:37.020
So I mean, there couldn't be anything more fun

1425
00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:39.330
and I love our organization and our team.

1426
00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:41.100
I've been a season ticket holder here in Atlanta

1427
00:55:41.100 --> 00:55:42.360
for a couple of years.

1428
00:55:42.360 --> 00:55:44.340
So I've been around the organization

1429
00:55:44.340 --> 00:55:47.070
and it's just such a pleasure and honor to work

1430
00:55:47.070 --> 00:55:49.920
with the players and the staff we have and the community.

1431
00:55:49.920 --> 00:55:50.970
It's great.

1432
00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:53.460
And we're new to this, we bought the team,

1433
00:55:53.460 --> 00:55:57.210
a small group of bunch of guys that I knew, friends,

1434
00:55:57.210 --> 00:55:59.850
and a couple guys here in Atlanta.

1435
00:55:59.850 --> 00:56:03.570
And we've only been on board for about three or four months,

1436
00:56:03.570 --> 00:56:05.190
so we're still learning.

1437
00:56:05.190 --> 00:56:06.540
There's a lot of work to do,

1438
00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:10.440
but I can't even explain it, it's just been phenomenal.

1439
00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:12.540
It's so crazy that like the guy

1440
00:56:12.540 --> 00:56:15.450
who's writing these theme songs for the teams.

1441
00:56:15.450 --> 00:56:18.000
I mean, if anyone would've said like this guy one day

1442
00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:20.790
is gonna be an owner of one of the franchises,

1443
00:56:20.790 --> 00:56:22.320
I mean, you can't script that, man.

1444
00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:23.153
No.

1445
00:56:23.153 --> 00:56:24.900
It's unbelievable, yeah
It's impossible.

1446
00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:26.457
It's impossible.
It's so crazy.

1447
00:56:26.457 --> 00:56:29.790
And lucky and fortunate and believe me,

1448
00:56:29.790 --> 00:56:31.320
I appreciate every minute of it

1449
00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:36.320
and it's been great, it's been great.

1450
00:56:36.420 --> 00:56:39.180
So what is it your involvement entail?

1451
00:56:39.180 --> 00:56:41.970
Like what does it look like in terms of your input

1452
00:56:41.970 --> 00:56:45.240
or lack of input or your relationship with the players

1453
00:56:45.240 --> 00:56:46.230
and all of that?

1454
00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:47.970
Well, because I'm here in Atlanta,

1455
00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:50.310
a big part of it is community related.

1456
00:56:50.310 --> 00:56:52.980
We've built five basketball courts in the inner cities

1457
00:56:52.980 --> 00:56:57.510
here in Atlanta recently and we have 25 more in the works.

1458
00:56:57.510 --> 00:57:00.990
And so a combination of community efforts,

1459
00:57:00.990 --> 00:57:02.808
a combination of some of the marking stuff,

1460
00:57:02.808 --> 00:57:05.760
I've done a lot of work in the past with the NBA.

1461
00:57:05.760 --> 00:57:08.700
So that's an area that I wanna focus on

1462
00:57:08.700 --> 00:57:11.072
and that I think we can get better in.

1463
00:57:11.072 --> 00:57:13.770
But all the basketball stuff,

1464
00:57:13.770 --> 00:57:15.720
we leave for the coaches and the coaching staff

1465
00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:18.120
and guys that have a lot more experience

1466
00:57:18.120 --> 00:57:21.330
and expertise than myself and my partners.

1467
00:57:21.330 --> 00:57:22.920
Well, these guys must know you have this book,

1468
00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:24.154
obviously, out, right?

1469
00:57:24.154 --> 00:57:24.987
Oh yeah.

1470
00:57:24.987 --> 00:57:29.490
So is your kind of like lifestyle habits wearing

1471
00:57:29.490 --> 00:57:31.200
off on any of the players?

1472
00:57:31.200 --> 00:57:35.010
You know, I'm lucky because one of the guys,

1473
00:57:35.010 --> 00:57:36.690
Kyle Korver, one of our players,

1474
00:57:36.690 --> 00:57:39.360
does a thing called the Musagi every year,

1475
00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:42.420
which is Japanese term, I don't know the exact specifics,

1476
00:57:42.420 --> 00:57:44.316
but basically you do something

1477
00:57:44.316 --> 00:57:47.370
that you do not think you could possibly finish

1478
00:57:47.370 --> 00:57:49.620
that can completely test your will

1479
00:57:49.620 --> 00:57:52.440
and challenge your soul, for example.

1480
00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:53.640
And this is a guy on our team.

1481
00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:54.473
Right.

1482
00:57:54.473 --> 00:57:55.306
So he loves-

1483
00:57:55.306 --> 00:57:56.139
It's like right up your alley, yeah.

1484
00:57:56.139 --> 00:57:57.870
Last year, he and a friend,

1485
00:57:57.870 --> 00:58:02.220
took, I believe it was like 60 or an 80 pound boulder

1486
00:58:02.220 --> 00:58:04.980
and dropped it in five feet of ocean water

1487
00:58:04.980 --> 00:58:08.190
and they would dive down one by one and move the boulder

1488
00:58:08.190 --> 00:58:09.210
for three miles.

1489
00:58:09.210 --> 00:58:10.530
It took him like 17 hours.

1490
00:58:10.530 --> 00:58:11.363
Oh wow.

1491
00:58:11.363 --> 00:58:14.220
And of course, there's all kinds of challenges,

1492
00:58:14.220 --> 00:58:16.350
physical fatigue, mental.

1493
00:58:16.350 --> 00:58:17.190
And every year, this guy.

1494
00:58:17.190 --> 00:58:20.340
So when I heard this story, I'm like, this is perfect.

1495
00:58:20.340 --> 00:58:21.390
Perfect for me.

1496
00:58:21.390 --> 00:58:24.180
So I gave Kyle the book and he loved it,

1497
00:58:24.180 --> 00:58:26.160
told me he started doing some of these push up

1498
00:58:26.160 --> 00:58:28.500
and pull-up challenges and this and that.

1499
00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:31.500
So the guys, look, I think they can relate

1500
00:58:31.500 --> 00:58:33.540
to the whole 100 mile experience

1501
00:58:33.540 --> 00:58:37.323
and what the preparation that it took and the will.

1502
00:58:38.190 --> 00:58:39.450
And they're professional athletes,

1503
00:58:39.450 --> 00:58:41.400
they go through their same challenges.

1504
00:58:41.400 --> 00:58:44.280
Like how do you get up for a game 80 times a year?

1505
00:58:44.280 --> 00:58:46.830
I mean, it's a lot, what these guys go through.

1506
00:58:46.830 --> 00:58:48.000
You don't realize it.

1507
00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:49.800
You say, "Oh, these guys are getting paid

1508
00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:51.960
to play basketball," but no, it's grueling.

1509
00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:54.600
It's physical, it's exhausting, there's travel,

1510
00:58:54.600 --> 00:58:57.210
there's other life stuff going on, family,

1511
00:58:57.210 --> 00:58:59.820
and they have to get up and perform every night.

1512
00:58:59.820 --> 00:59:03.705
And we have a team of guys that do that.

1513
00:59:03.705 --> 00:59:05.520
I mean, they're diving for loose balls

1514
00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:07.080
and jumping in the bleachers.

1515
00:59:07.080 --> 00:59:08.880
So it's great to see.

1516
00:59:08.880 --> 00:59:10.860
Have you ever thought about bringing David out

1517
00:59:10.860 --> 00:59:12.150
to talk with the guys?

1518
00:59:12.150 --> 00:59:13.020
Absolutely.

1519
00:59:13.020 --> 00:59:15.390
They all know the story and it's in the works

1520
00:59:15.390 --> 00:59:16.713
and he be a great motivator for these guys.

1521
00:59:16.713 --> 00:59:18.630
I love that, man, that's fantastic.

1522
00:59:18.630 --> 00:59:19.463
Very cool.

1523
00:59:19.463 --> 00:59:21.420
All right, well, you're married

1524
00:59:21.420 --> 00:59:25.170
to an amazingly successful entrepreneur.

1525
00:59:25.170 --> 00:59:28.440
Between the two of you, you have very rich business lives,

1526
00:59:28.440 --> 00:59:30.032
you also have three kids.

1527
00:59:30.032 --> 00:59:33.390
How do you like find that work life balance

1528
00:59:33.390 --> 00:59:35.640
in terms of making sure that you're attending

1529
00:59:35.640 --> 00:59:36.570
to your relationship?

1530
00:59:36.570 --> 00:59:37.920
You know, she's busy, you're busy,

1531
00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:39.510
you're doing all these things.

1532
00:59:39.510 --> 00:59:42.600
You've got this great family and young kids.

1533
00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:43.830
So how does that work?

1534
00:59:43.830 --> 00:59:46.325
Well, I'm not gonna lie, it's hard, you know?

1535
00:59:46.325 --> 00:59:48.900
I would be lying to say that it's not challenging.

1536
00:59:48.900 --> 00:59:51.332
Sarah's got her business and a lot of responsibilities

1537
00:59:51.332 --> 00:59:52.680
and her foundation,

1538
00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:55.110
which is a lot bigger than my foundation.

1539
00:59:55.110 --> 00:59:58.620
And trying to manage all of this stuff

1540
00:59:58.620 --> 01:00:00.510
with our kids is hard,

1541
01:00:00.510 --> 01:00:04.470
but we're very mindful of our time,

1542
01:00:04.470 --> 01:00:06.210
we make sure that we have a date night

1543
01:00:06.210 --> 01:00:07.380
or date nights every week,

1544
